# Document Auto-Crop (crop-documents)

Detects, crops, and de-skews photos of documents/receipts/cards with a natural background
margin, optional alignment/rotation, and PDF bundling. Two front ends — a **macOS CLI** and
an **iPhone app** — over one shared Swift engine built on Apple Vision + CoreImage.

## Module map

| Path | Role |
|------|------|
| `ios/DocumentCropCore/Sources/DocumentCropCore` | **The engine.** Detect, warp, deskew, trim, orient, enhance, image IO, PDF |
| `ios/DocumentCropCore/Sources/CropDocumentsCLI` | Mac front end: arg parsing, file walk, console output |
| `ios/Margin` | iPhone SwiftUI front end (pick / camera → crop → Photos) |
| `lab/` | Regression cases + variant runner (`run_regression.sh`) |

## Pipeline

load → Vision detect → expand quad → warp → optional `--deskew` (align + flap trim)
→ optional `--rotate` (quarter turn) → optional `--enhance` → save (HEIC→JPEG)

## Agent rules of engagement

- **Fix pixels in `DocumentCropCore`, never in a front end.** Both platforms consume the
  same engine; a fix applied in `ios/Margin` or the CLI is a fix that silently doesn't
  ship to the other. The front ends only pick files and show results.
- **Re-run `cd ios && xcodegen generate` after adding any core source file.** SPM globs the
  directory but the checked-in `.xcodeproj` enumerates files, so skipping this leaves
  `swift build` green while the iOS app fails to compile.
- **Verify both front ends** after touching the core: `swift build --package-path
  ios/DocumentCropCore` and `xcodebuild -project ios/Margin.xcodeproj -scheme Margin
  -destination 'generic/platform=iOS Simulator' build`.
- **Platform-specific tuning belongs in a policy type, not in shared code.** See
  `DetectionPolicy` — iOS needs a strict area floor to reject sub-region latches on camera
  photos; Mac needs a lenient one to match Python on curated files.
- **The trim's edge detector is delicate** — `cannyLite` has broken twice (hysteresis read
  as a band; missing non-maximum suppression), and `analysisMaxSide` below 1600 closes the
  void between flap and page. Validate trim changes with `./lab/run_regression.sh` and the
  `probe-trim` subcommand, not by eyeballing one image.
- Keep the public CLI simple: **`--deskew`** (align) and **`--rotate`** (quarter turn) —
  not a matrix of experimental flags.
- macOS 13+, Xcode CLT. Run `./build-cli.sh` to build the CLI. No Python, OpenCV, or
  Tesseract — the pipeline is Vision + CoreImage + CoreGraphics only.

## Current state

- The Python/OpenCV pipeline was removed on 2026-08-07 after the Swift engine matched it
  on both the default crop path and `--deskew`. `lab/python-reference-baseline.json`
  records the dimensions it produced.
- `--rotate`: sideways pages only; deliberately does not flip 180° (Vision reads
  upside-down text nearly as well as upright — see `DocumentOrienter`).
- `straighten` is off by default and hidden in the iOS UI pending on-device re-verification.
- Diagnostics: `probe-trim`, `probe-skew`, `probe-orientation` report what each stage
  decided without modifying anything.
